Note on the "All rights reserved" metadata
------------------------------------------
The upstream jar released on Modrinth/CurseForge carries the unmodified Forge
template default `license="All rights reserved"` in its META-INF/mods.toml.
That value contradicts the upstream repository's LICENCE file (MIT) and the
Modrinth project page (MIT). The repository LICENCE file is the operative
license grant, so this derivative build records `license="MIT"` in mods.toml
to match it.

Modifications made in this derivative
-------------------------------------
1. build.gradle
   - `archivesBaseName` changed from "woodencog" to "woodencog_wildfire".
     This affects only the output jar file name.
   - `processResources` now uses `duplicatesStrategy = EXCLUDE`, so the
     hand-written resources win over the placeholder lang file datagen emits
     for the same path. This matches what the upstream release ships.

2. src/main/resources/META-INF/mods.toml
   - displayName changed from "Wooden Cog" to "Wooden Cog: Wildfire"
   - license changed from "All rights reserved" to "MIT" (see note above)
   - credits extended with upstream attribution and a non-affiliation statement
   - description extended with a derivative-build note

3. Added src/main/resources/META-INF/LICENCE (verbatim upstream MIT text)
   and this NOTICE file, so the required notices travel inside the jar.

4. compat/createmoreburners/CMBIntegrationImpl.java - native integration.
   The More Burners electric burner used to be patched from outside by the
   Wildfires mod (WoodenCogMoreBurnerTemperatureMixin), which mapped the block's
   Create heat level onto the configured thresholds and ramped the temperature
   with TFC's device-temperature adjustment. That behaviour is now Wooden Cog's
   own code, reading WoodenCogCommonConfigs directly instead of by reflection.
   The Wildfires mixin has been disabled. Other More Burners blocks keep the
   original linear formula.

5. item/WoodencogItems.java - removed a registration nothing used.
   Upstream registered an "unfinished" twin of every TFC metal item here, by
   iterating TFCItems.METAL_ITEMS - roughly 477 items named <tfc path>/unfinished.
   No recipe, tag, loot table or creative tab referenced them, they had no
   translation entries (so they showed as raw keys such as
   item.woodencog.metal.knife_blade.black_bronze.unfinished), and 240 of them had
   no model either. This build drops the registration so they no longer clutter
   the JEI item list. It came in with upstream commit a18dd48 ("New Test Block,
   Item"), and reinstating the loop from upstream 1.2.17 restores them.

6. An eighth, superheated charcoal forge heat tier -- new feature.
   TFC's shared heat_level block state property stops at 7, so a forge could
   never look hotter than white hot, and everything sitting on top of one - the
   crucible, and the Create machines read through mixin/heat/MixinBasinBlockEntity
   - had no way to tell a merely white-hot forge from a genuinely superheated one.
   This build adds tier 8:

   - mixin/heat/MixinTFCBlockStateProperties.java raises the maximum of TFC's
     heat_level property from 7 to 8. TFC declares 26 IntegerProperty instances
     inside a single static initialiser, so the redirect matches on the runtime
     property name rather than on the call site.
   - mixin/heat/MixinCharcoalForgeBlockEntity.java promotes a lit forge to tier 8
     once its device temperature reaches 2300 C. It presents tier 8 to TFC's own
     ticker as tier 7, so the vanilla Mth.clamp(..., 1, 7) neither fights the
     value nor performs a second block update per tick, and it is the single
     writer of tier 8. The superheated tier is therefore stable, not flickering.
   - utils/CogUtil.java: the reverse mapping from a heat level back to a
     temperature now spans nine tiers instead of eight (divisor 6 -> 7), which
     moves the superheated tier from level 7 to level 8. Level 7 is still white
     hot, it is simply no longer treated as superheated. That is deliberate.
   - New resources: assets/tfc/blockstates/charcoal_forge.json (TFC's eight
     variants plus heat_level=8), assets/woodencog/models/block/charcoal_forge/
     heat_8.json, and the matching top-face texture with its .mcmeta.

   This was previously injected from outside by the Wildfires mod. Wildfires'
   TFCBlockStatePropertiesMixin is now disabled and its two charcoal forge heat
   injections have been removed, because the heat_level property must have exactly
   one owner - two mods extending it would install two redirects on the same
   IntegerProperty.create call site, and two writers of the same block state would
   make the model flicker. Wildfires' own earthen oven keeps its heat_level=8
   variants and now relies on this mod to provide the extended property.

7. Fan heat ceilings and a fan overheat guard.
   Create's encased fan heats an item towards a ceiling, and this mod used to
   hard-code both of them: 1700 C with lava behind the fan, 200 C with fire, a
   campfire or a lit TFC firepit behind it. They are now read from
   woodencog-common.toml under [woodencog.fan]:

   - blasting_temperature defaults to 1500.0 (upstream 1700.0). 1500 stays below
     the 1535/1540 melting point of wrought iron, cast iron and every steel, so a
     fan no longer melts those away; copper, bronze, brass, nickel and black steel
     still melt as before.
   - smoking_temperature defaults to 600.0 (upstream 200.0).
   - overheat_multiplier (1.5) and no_recipe_ceiling (1600.0) bound the new guard
     in compat/create/FanOverheatGuard.java, which mixin/MixinFanOverheat.java
     injects into Create's FanProcessing at priority 900, ahead of this mod's own
     priority 1000 heating injector:
       * an item that defines a welding temperature is destroyed above
         welding_temperature * overheat_multiplier. The multiplier must not go
         below 1.25: every TFC metal melts at 1.25 times its welding temperature,
         so a smaller value would destroy the metal before it is able to melt.
       * an item with no welding temperature and no heating recipe would otherwise
         heat forever, so it is destroyed above no_recipe_ceiling. Because a
         blasting fan on its own now stops at 1500, this clause only fires for an
         item that was already above 1600 when it entered the fan; set it to 0.0 to
         switch it off.
     Neither injection cancels while an item is below its ceiling, so the ordinary
     heating, cooking and melting paths are untouched.

   Fixed at the same time: the dropped-item injection used to cancel Create's own
   fan processing whenever TFC's heating recipe had nothing to apply yet. Because
   CallbackInfoReturnable.setReturnValue also cancels, an item carrying both TFC
   heat data and a Create splashing recipe could never be washed while lying on
   the ground, even though the same item washed normally on a belt or a depot
   (that path only cancels when the TFC heating recipe actually changed the
   stack). The dropped-item injection now cancels only for lava and fire fans,
   where this mod deliberately takes the item over; a water fan just cools the
   item and hands it back to Create, so washing works again.

   The guard and its two values were previously injected from outside by the
   Wildfires mod (first.wildfires.mixin.create.FanOverheatMixin,
   first.wildfires.compat.create.FanOverheatGuard, wildfires-fan-heat.toml, which
   defaulted to overheatMultiplier 1.3 and noRecipeCeiling 1600.0 and which the
   pack raised to 1.5). Every item a fan heats passes through this mod, so the fan
   now has a single owner. Wildfires' mixin and its config registration are
   disabled, and wildfires-fan-heat.toml is no longer created and is inert where it
   already exists. Nothing about the guard's behaviour changed other than the two
   ceilings named above and where its values are read from.

   blaze_burner_seething now defaults to 2300.0 instead of 2000.0, to match
   Create's own superheated temperature and the threshold at which a charcoal forge
   is promoted to heat tier 8 by item 6. The pack config already set 2300.0, so no
   world sees a change from that default.

Mod id and compatibility
------------------------
The mod id remains "woodencog". No Java package, registry name or mixin config
was renamed, and no asset or data namespace was rewritten. This build is a
drop-in replacement for the upstream woodencog 1.2.x jar on Minecraft 1.20.1 /
Forge, and is save-compatible with worlds that already used woodencog.

The only registry change is the removal described in item 5 above. Those items
were not obtainable through gameplay, so no legitimate save contains them.

Item 6 extends an existing block state property instead of adding a block. A
forge only ever stored values 0 to 7, so no existing world holds a value the
extended property cannot represent, and no migration is needed. The reverse is
not true: heat_level=8 has to be known to the client, so every client that joins
a server running this build needs this mod as well. A vanilla TFC client cannot
represent the tier and will fail to load that block state definition.

